[White "4) Central Control"]
[Black "Central Control: Introduction"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r1b2rk1/1pp2pp1/2n1pq1p/p2p4/2PP4/PPQ1PN2/5PPP/R3KB1R b KQ - 0 11"]

 { Central control is closely linked with space advantage, as it is characterised by a favorable pawn formation in the centre. That said, they are not the same thing. In the case of central control, we must also pay special attention to the piece activity in the centre. It might be the case that the pawn structure is symmetrical, but one side's pieces are exerting more influence on the centre. In such a case, that side would have superior central control without having a space advantage. That said, the majority of the examples we'll be covering in this chapter will also involve a space advantage as in most situations the two do go hand in hand. This chapter will be divided into three sections: 1. The power of the centre: how to use central control to your advantage 2. Fighting against the centre: methods for the defending side to fight against the centre 3. Protecting the centre: ways in which you can protect the centre to make it as difficult as possible for your opponent to undermine it. Let's begin by covering a classic game, which Jose Raul Capablanca won with the White pieces due to his opponent's failure to contest the centre. } 11... { -KEY- } Bd7 $6 { Black's 11th move is an early sign that he is not aware of the risk of falling behind in the battle for the centre. In this case, we see that there is a symmetrical structure on the central files (e3/d4 pawns for White, e6/d5 pawns for Black). However, White has their c-pawn on c4 and so if we consider the expanded centre as the c- to f-files, White is leading the race. } ( 11... dxc4 $1 { This is the move that you would expect a modern Grandmaster to play almost 100% of the time. At first glance it seems counterintuitive, as Black is trading off their most central pawn for, in effect, White's pawn on b3. However, there is a concrete follow-up in mind: } 12. bxc4 Re8 $1 { Black is looking to break with ...e5, undermining White's centre and at the same time activating the scope of their bishop. } 13. Be2 e5 $1 14. d5 { White is forced to advance, but now Black has weakened White's control over the dark squares. } Nb8 $1 { The knight retreats to its starting square in order to navigate to the newly created weak square on c5. } 15. O-O b6 $1 { First Black takes the sting out of c4-c5 advances. } ( 15... Nd7 $6 { would allow White to spoil Black's plans with the move } 16. c5 $14 ) 
16. c5 { is probably best, but here Black can rely on the other typical strategy when dealing with inferior central control and/or space problems -- exchanging pieces! } Ba6 $1 17. Rfe1 bxc5 18. Qxc5 Qd6 $1 { A temporary pawn sacrifice. } 19. Qxa5 Nd7 20. Bxa6 Rxa6 21. Qb5 Rea8 $11 { Black will pick up the pawn on a3 next, and their compact pawn chain and solid piece coordination assures them equality in spite of the isolated pawn on c7. This elimination of risk is the type of thing that top-flight Grandmasters have become so adept at, and a major driver of the high number of draws in elite tournaments. } ) 
12. Bd3 Rfc8 $6 { Black continues to play passively, and with this move signals that he is not on the lookout for ideas like ...dxc4 followed by preparing the ...e5 break. } 13. O-O a4 14. b4 { Sidestepping the pawn advance. In the future the a4 pawn may become an easy target. } dxc4 { Black finally breaks the tension in the centre, but now it is too late since there is no convincing follow-up. } 15. Bxc4 Na7 16. Ne5 $6 { An imprecise move, better was to play 16.Qd3! to snuff out Black's strongest plan. } ( 16. Qd3 $1 { This move would have stopped ...Bb5 ideas. } ) 
Be8 $2 ( 16... Bb5 $1 { Black needed to exchange some pieces to reduce the effect of White's greater central control and space advantage. } 17. Rfc1 Bxc4 18. Qxc4 c6 19. Qc2 Nb5 $11 { The position is close to equal since, in spite of White's space advantage and superior central control, there is no clear path forward as Black can lean on his healthy piece coordination and the valuable b5 outpost to maintain a dynamic balance. } ) 
17. f4 b6 18. Qd3 Rd8 $2 { This move loses on the spot, but already the defensive task was close to futile. } 19. f5 $1 { A devastating pawn break. White has not had to break a sweat to establish a winning position. } b5 20. fxe6 { Forcing favorable simplifications. } ( 20. Ba2 $1 { was even stronger. Perhaps Capablanca rejected this as at first glance it appears to lose a piece. However, White has a clever point if Black grabs the knight: } Qxe5 21. fxe6 $1 fxe6 22. Bb1 $1 { A beautiful idea, threatening mate on h7 and simultaneously defending the queen. } g6 23. dxe5 Rxd3 24. Bxd3 $18 { White emerges an exchange to the good, with an easily winning position. One of the most effective ways of converting a positional advantage is to lure the opponent into tactical ideas that do not quite work. Often, even if they see the refutation, they will play into the line anyway out of desperation and in the hope that you have not spotted the winning idea. } ) 
bxc4 21. Rxf6 cxd3 22. exf7+ Bxf7 23. Rxf7 { The dust has settled, and White is up one pawn for now with a second likely to be picked up soon. From here, Capablanca quickly secures the full point. } Nb5 24. Rf2 Rd5 25. Nxd3 Re8 26. Rf3 $18 { Black resigned in Capablanca,J - Mikenas,V (1939). This game illustrates the dire consequences that can result when one side does not combat a central advantage in time. White ran no risk at any point in the game, and did not have to find any difficult ideas to guide the game to victory. We can see that in this game White used his central advantage to quickly switch to a kingside attack. We'll be exploring this link between the centre & attacking chances next. } 
